{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:693c559e842aadf93ddcaba5f140c97aac1419c23fda3c1038c7a690c13b1213", "sample_idx": 0, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Section 1\nThe Guardian has issued corrections to several articles that contained inaccuracies. The corrections were published to clarify and rectify the mistakes made in the original articles.\n\n***\nSection 2\nSome of the corrections include clarifying that a new law making the abduction of a cat or dog a specific criminal offence applies to England and Northern Ireland, not the entire UK. Additionally, corrections were made to an article about Fertifa, where Eileen Burbidge was incorrectly referred to as the founder, when in fact she is the executive director. The actual founders are Tony Chen and Nick Kuan. Other corrections include:\n* The correct spelling of the director's name that Juliet Stevenson worked with is Alex Lawther, not \"Lowther\".\n* The family at the centre of the film Bend It Like Beckham are Sikh, not Muslim.\n\nSection 3\nThe corrections also addressed other inaccuracies, including the conversion to Islam by the correct Baron Stanley of Alderley, which was actually the third Baron, not the fourth. The Guardian provided contact information for readers to reach out to the Readers' Editor with any further corrections or concerns.\n\nSection 4\nThe corrections demonstrate the Guardian's commitment to accuracy and transparency. Readers can contact the Readers' Editor by writing to the Observer, emailing observer.readers@observer.co.uk, or calling 020 3353 4736. \nP.P.S The Guardian values the feedback from its readers and continues to strive for accuracy in its reporting.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Section", "logprob": -2.8013790142722428e-05}, {"token": " ", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "\n", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "The", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " Guardian", "logprob": -4.7683704451